messages:
olivia_support: Customer reported truncated response body after a 503 retry -> ended up billing for a partial request and the app saw a 400. They say it only happens on retries from our TS SDK in Node 18.

mike_dev: ugh. do we have repro? which SDK version, undici, node?

olivia_support: customer provided stack: node v18.17.1, @redwood/inference v1.3.2, undici 5.17.0. They reproduced locally with this tiny script:

olivia_support: ```ts
import fetch from 'node-fetch'; // they used global fetch via undici in their app
const url = 'https://api.redwood.example/generate';
const body = JSON.stringify({prompt: 'hello'.repeat(10000)});
const ac = new AbortController();

async function send() {
  // SDK-ish: reusing same AbortController across retries
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, {method: 'POST', body, signal: ac.signal});
    console.log(await res.text());
  } catch (e) {
    // SDK does retry here
    // retry 1: reuses same body buffer/stream and same signal
  }
}
send();
```

olivia_support: They said the first attempt times out mid-upload on large payload (500ms), undici aborts connection. Retry happens but the server sees a truncated Content-Length vs actual bytes -> 400 or connection closed, and request bill counted once with no idempotency key so customer saw duplicate inference charges when retry succeeded with partial earlier attempt.

lee_infra: interesting. undici historically had issues with reusing body streams across retries — if body is a stream it can't be replayed. But they passed a string so it should be fine. Could be the SDK is streaming via a Readable that becomes consumed on first try.

ashley_eng: which part indicates SDK-created stream reuse? any stack traces from the runtime?

olivia_support: they included logs. excerpt:

olivia_support: ```log
[client] request attempt=0 sending body length=125000
[undici] socket error: request aborted by signal
[client] retrying attempt=1 (backoff 200ms)
[client] request attempt=1 sending body length=125000
[server] received body length=82345
[server] respond 400 Bad Request
```

mike_dev: server saw shorter length on second attempt -> suggests chunking/connection closed mid-send and undici didn't re-buffer the body properly. Also see note about Retry-After header and our retry-window logic.

lee_infra: what SDK retry settings? backoff jitter? any header rewriting like Transfer-Encoding: chunked vs Content-Length toggles?

olivia_support: SDK config default: retries=2, backoff base=100ms, jitter=true. They also used keepAlive agent via undici Client with pipelining.

ashley_eng: potential culprit: when using undici Client.request with body as a stream or iterable, undici may stream and release the body on abort; retries need a fresh body or buffer. Our SDK should clone the body (Buffer.from(body)) for retries if small, or error if non-replayable. Also need to create a fresh AbortController per attempt; reusing the same signal that has been aborted would immediately cancel retry.

mike_dev: good call about AbortController reuse. If we reuse the same AC and it was aborted on attempt 0, then attempt 1 will be aborted immediately. But logs show attempt=1 proceeded and sent >0 bytes. So AC probably recreated. The truncated length suggests body stream not replayed.

olivia_support: customer says they pass a Node Readable (file upload) in real world. reproduction used string to simplify.

ashley_eng: OK action plan:
1) ask customer to try SDK nightly that buffers small bodies before retry (workaround)
2) add temporary warning docs: do not pass non-replayable streams unless you supply an idempotency key
3) create a failing unit test reproducing the truncated-length scenario with undici 5.17.0 and Node 18.

lee_infra: also test undici 5.20.0 + Node 20. There were fixes around streaming + retries in later undici versions.

mike_dev: minimal patch idea: when retrying, if body is a string/Buffer/TypedArray — clone and reuse; if body is stream/async iterable, do not retry and surface a clear error ("non-replayable body, set retry=0 or provide idempotency-key"). That avoids silent truncation and billing surprises.

olivia_support: shared repro script that uses a simple express server to intentionally kill connection mid-body to mimic customer:

olivia_support: ```js
// server
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.post('/generate', (req, res) => {
  let received = 0;
  req.on('data', (chunk) => { received += chunk.length; if (received > 50000) req.destroy(); });
  req.on('close', () => { /* closed by us */ });
});
app.listen(3000);
```

ashley_eng: nice repro. I'll try with undici 5.17.0 and then 5.20.0. If newer undici fixes it, we can recommend upgrade in support doc.

deploy-bot: Deployment queued: feature/avoid-retries-nonreplayable-bodies -> staging (priority: low)

mike_dev: also check our HTTP wrapper that injects the AbortController. Make sure it creates new AC per attempt and sets appropriate timeouts rather than relying on external signal reuse.

olivia_support: Customer update: asked them to patch SDK config to retries=0 and add idempotency-key; they confirmed temporary mitigation avoided duplicate billing. They're ok to test nightly.

lee_infra: next steps: reproduce, write unit tests, patch SDK to detect non-replayable bodies, doc change + support KB, and a changelog entry if we bump undici requirement.

ashley_eng: I'll own the PR for the SDK patch. mike, can you add the unit test and CI matrix for undici 5.17/5.20 + node 18/20?

mike_dev: yup I'll add tests.

olivia_support: thanks team — I'll update the customer thread with mitigation steps and ETA for patch. :thumbsup:
